<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankfurt Parliament</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frankfurt National Assembly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> Parliament was the first freely elected all-German assembly, meeting in St Paul's Church in Frankfurt from May 1848 during the revolutions of that year. It drafted a liberal constitution for a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and offered the imperial crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>, who refused it in 1849. The assembly then broke up.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament.jpg" alt="Frankfurt Parliament" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Coat_of_arms_of_Germany_(1848).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The assembly was part of the outcome of the March Revolution in the states of the German Confederation. Opposition politicians demanded basic and civic rights, liberal governments in the states and above all a German nation-state with a pan-German constitution and a popular assembly. On 5 March 1848 they met at the Heidelberg Assembly and resolved to form a pre-parliament, which sat in St Paul's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> from 31 March to 3 April. It decided to cooperate with the Confederate Diet in forming a national constitutional assembly, with one deputy for every 50,000 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The assembly was elected on 1 May 1848 and opened on 18 May in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-pauls-church-frankfurt">Paulskirche</a>, with 379 deputies present. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-gagern">Heinrich von Gagern</a> was elected president. Because it had not been created by the German Confederation, it had no head of state, no government and no legal legitimation, and powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> declined to recognise it. On 28 June it voted for a Provisional Central Power and the next day chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John of Austria</a> as Regent of the Empire. In November 1848 the Austrian army executed the deputy Robert Blum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, despite his parliamentary immunity, which showed how dependent the assembly was on the goodwill of the states.</p><p>The deputies began drafting the constitution on 19 October 1848. It passed on 27 March 1849 by 267 votes to 263, with a hereditary emperor, a directly elected lower house and a House of the States. On 28 March the assembly chose the Prussian king as emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Frederick William IV refused the crown, on the grounds that the constitution and the offer abridged the rights of the princes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> rejected the constitution on 21 April 1849. The Austrian deputies had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 5 April, and Gagern resigned on 10 May. Revolts followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, the Palatinate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. On 31 May the remaining deputies decided to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, and from 6 June a rump parliament of 154 deputies met there. On 18 June the Württemberg army occupied the chamber and expelled the deputies from other states. In the 20th century major elements of the Frankfurt constitution became models for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a> of 1919 and the Basic Law of 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frankfurt National Assembly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1848 the Diet yielded its authority to the Imperial Regent, Archduke John of Austria, appointed by the <b>Frankfurt Parliament</b>, but it was restored in 1850–51 after the failure of the revolution and Prussia’s retreat at Olmütz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1848 the liberal Heinrich von Gagern became chief minister before he was elected president of the <b>Frankfurt National Assembly</b>, and Louis III succeeded the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 1849 Württemberg soldiers dispersed the rump of the <b>Frankfurt National Assembly</b>, which had moved to Stuttgart.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1848 some Limburgers sought separation from the Netherlands to join Germany, and deputies sat in the <b>Frankfurt Parliament</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-limburg-1839-1867">Duchy of Limburg (1839-1867)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1848-49 the <b>Frankfurt Parliament</b>, the first freely elected German national assembly, met in St Paul's Church and drafted a constitution for a united Germany.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-city-of-frankfurt">Free City of Frankfurt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1815 it became a free city, seat of the German Confederation and of the 1848 <b>Frankfurt Parliament</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-frankfurt">Free Imperial City of Frankfurt</a></li></ul></section>
